##invisible learning labor detailed
 -information navigation(Ⅰ)
 -pathway planning(Ⅰ)
 -resource evaluation(Ⅱ)
 -task balance(Ⅱ)
 -mental pressure(Ⅱ)
 -self-proving(Ⅲ)
 *Invisible learning labor is distinguished from direct learning effort.

##types of support system
 | Formal Support | alternative support | Conceptual Type      |
 | -------------- | ------------------- | -------------------- |
 | High           | Low                 | Public provision     |
 | Low            | High                | Market substitution  |
 | High           | High                | Cumulative advantage |
 | Low            | Low                 | Support gap          |
 *Similar overall levels of functional support may compose of structurally different sources.

##Hypothesized relationships
 -support level is negatively associated with invidible learning labor, in other words, formal support gap is positively associated with invisible learning labor.
 -formal support gap is positively associated with the need for alternative support.
 -students with greater compensatory capacity are more able to transform subtitle resources into effective support.
 -effective alternative support is negatively associated with remaining support gap, but the process of obtaining and managing it itself can generate new invisible learning labor.
 -formal support gap is positively associated with the amount of invisible learning labor needed, which then negatively effects the time and cognitive resource for direct learning.
 -similar standardized test outcoomes may conceal different support system, alternative support pathways and different levels of invisible learning labor. Thus, they can't be interpreted equally.

##conceptual boundaries
 -accessible support ≠ actual support
 -the amount of support received ≠ support quality
 -direct learning effort ≠ invisible learning labor
 -amount of money spent on alternative support ≠ quality of alternative support
 -digital access ≠ digital literacy
 -observed association ≠ causal effect
